WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:04.680
Hi, this is Steve Kaufmann and I'm,
again, and I'm really pleased to have with

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:09.840
me today as a guest, someone that I've
known for a long time at various sort of

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:17.240
meetups of language keeners Olly Richards
of storylearning.com and um hello Olly.

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:18.150
Hi, Steve.

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:22.800
Please allow me to say, first of
all, hi, if people, uh, enjoy my

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.710
videos, please subscribe, click
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:28.470
If you follow me on a podcast,
please leave a comment.

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:29.730
Olly.

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:30.549
Steve

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:35.760
uh, you and I have been involved in this
whole kind of internet based language

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:37.560
learning thing for quite a while.

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:42.840
And I want to go over some of the ways
in which language learning has changed.

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:46.019
And of course, both of us started
learning languages long before this

00:00:46.019 --> 00:00:47.610
whole internet thing started...

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:50.970
how language learning has
changed, how the technology has

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:53.280
changed, how we've had to change.

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:56.040
So that's what I would like to
talk about with you if you agree.

00:00:56.879 --> 00:00:58.290
I am at your service.

00:01:00.404 --> 00:01:00.855
Okay.

00:01:01.095 --> 00:01:05.695
So, uh, before we get going, tell me
a little bit about storylearning.com.

00:01:06.315 --> 00:01:06.465
Yeah.

00:01:06.465 --> 00:01:11.205
So storylearning is, um, my website,
my YouTube channel, and the whole

00:01:11.205 --> 00:01:15.195
focus of what we do is that we
teach languages through stories.

00:01:15.195 --> 00:01:18.074
And this all came about,
um, many moons ago.

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:23.190
Um, I mean, I've always used stories
and reading as a way to learn languages.

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:28.260
Um, but I never really thought about how
I could use it as a teaching vehicle.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:32.280
Um, but then I had a long career
teaching languages and then I began

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:35.630
to actually write books of short
stories, which people really love.

00:01:36.255 --> 00:01:38.595
And then, so I kind of developed
this concept and thought, well,

00:01:38.615 --> 00:01:41.205
how many different ways can we
teach people through stories?

00:01:41.735 --> 00:01:46.015
Much the same as the mini stories
at LingQ, um, uh, but now inserting

00:01:46.045 --> 00:01:47.745
pedagogical elements as well.

00:01:47.985 --> 00:01:50.505
Um, and so nowadays that's,
that's everything that we do.

00:01:50.505 --> 00:01:53.265
So we make courses, we write books
and everything is about stories.

00:01:56.415 --> 00:01:56.835
Okay.

00:01:57.435 --> 00:02:02.445
And before we go further into that, of
course telling stories in a way, our

00:02:02.445 --> 00:02:04.875
presence on YouTube is a form of story.

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:07.500
Uh, we talk about different things.

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:08.340
We tell stories.

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:11.760
And I just wanted to, I mentioned
this to you before we started

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:13.470
up, you know, here we are.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:18.240
Uh, I've been in this business of, I
mean, I guess my first YouTube video

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:19.770
was, I don't know, 15 years ago.

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:20.770
I remember it well.

00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:21.380
I was just thinking....

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:22.100
On the chair.

00:02:22.970 --> 00:02:25.079
Yeah on the chair.

00:02:25.125 --> 00:02:28.695
So, so then I was just thinking
here I am, you know, getting set

00:02:28.695 --> 00:02:30.195
up for my interview with you.

00:02:30.195 --> 00:02:32.685
And I thought I would describe
to people what I have to do.

00:02:33.135 --> 00:02:37.065
So I have my iPad open because
that's where I communicate with you.

00:02:37.505 --> 00:02:41.745
And you might describe what you do at
your end, but so I, I'm no longer looking

00:02:41.745 --> 00:02:43.394
at you, even though you are there.

00:02:43.394 --> 00:02:48.975
And I hear you through my Air Pods
because in fact, I'm recording the

00:02:48.975 --> 00:02:55.230
video on my iPhone, which is located
about six feet away from me on a tripod.

00:02:56.100 --> 00:03:02.130
Now the iPhone is connected into my
computer because I have my computer

00:03:02.399 --> 00:03:08.549
open beside my iPad and plugged into my
computer is first of all, a microphone.

00:03:09.179 --> 00:03:13.350
And second of all is a cable
that takes me back to my iPhone.

00:03:14.055 --> 00:03:18.555
And then I use Quick Time and I use
a program on Quick Time to make sure

00:03:18.555 --> 00:03:22.305
that I'm centered more or less centered
because I tend to be a bit sloppy in

00:03:22.305 --> 00:03:27.615
whatever I do, but I got, I can see on
my computer screen, how I am centered,

00:03:27.885 --> 00:03:29.685
what actually will show up in the video.

00:03:29.715 --> 00:03:34.245
So I have to get that going, now the
audio from my iPhone because it's

00:03:34.245 --> 00:03:36.765
six feet away, wouldn't be very good.

00:03:36.915 --> 00:03:42.644
So I have my microphone plugged into my
computer and I use Audacity to record

00:03:43.545 --> 00:03:48.645
the, uh, audio, and then we merged
the audio and the video files in order

00:03:48.645 --> 00:03:51.885
to get a good quality video on audio.

00:03:52.035 --> 00:03:57.195
So we are not at all dependent on the
connection that we have through Zoom,

00:03:57.555 --> 00:04:01.755
because although Zoom and Skype have
improved there can be interruptions

00:04:01.755 --> 00:04:05.565
and frozen moments and, you know,
different audio quality and so forth.

00:04:05.835 --> 00:04:09.145
One other thing is for whatever
strange reason, every time I open

00:04:09.145 --> 00:04:11.580
Audacity I get an error message.

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:16.079
So I always have to go through the
same step of quitting Audacity,

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:18.010
searching for it, with that...

00:04:18.010 --> 00:04:21.780
Magnifying glass to get it up again.

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:24.510
And then the second time I
get into Audacity, it works.

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:26.700
So just some background.

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:27.060
Oh.

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:30.810
And I have three different sets of
lights here to improve the light.

00:04:32.114 --> 00:04:36.284
So there's a whole set up process
to do these, to tell my story.

00:04:37.335 --> 00:04:38.275
How about at your end?

00:04:38.294 --> 00:04:41.265
That's the interesting thing about
that whole setup you've got there

00:04:41.265 --> 00:04:42.585
is it didn't begin like that.

00:04:42.585 --> 00:04:44.655
It's is something that's evolved
over the, over the years.

00:04:44.655 --> 00:04:49.844
And as I say, there's so many
parallels between this and other

00:04:49.844 --> 00:04:53.265
things in life where you just start
off, you know, your very first video.

00:04:53.265 --> 00:04:56.445
I believe it was a web cam on your
computer with you just sitting on a chair.

00:04:56.475 --> 00:04:58.425
And that was, that was that same for me.

00:04:58.995 --> 00:05:01.095
Um, but then as we go,
we add more things on.

00:05:01.095 --> 00:05:07.755
So my setup is at the moment,
so we are talking on Zoom.

00:05:08.055 --> 00:05:08.955
That's how we're doing it.

00:05:09.255 --> 00:05:13.215
And right now I'm looking at the
call itself into the laptop, which

00:05:13.215 --> 00:05:16.785
nobody can see, but I can't record the
laptop because the camera is no good.

00:05:17.595 --> 00:05:22.275
So what I do is I have my fancy DSLR
camera, which I use for my YouTube videos,

00:05:22.455 --> 00:05:25.424
um, set up above the screen up here.

00:05:25.994 --> 00:05:30.525
So I'm looking at the camera right now
because that's how we get a nice effect

00:05:30.554 --> 00:05:32.895
for the audience who watches it later.

00:05:33.614 --> 00:05:34.544
But I can't see your face.

00:05:34.544 --> 00:05:36.674
Your face is down here and I'm
looking at the camera up here

00:05:37.275 --> 00:05:39.075
and then linked to my camera.

00:05:39.135 --> 00:05:46.245
I have an overhead mic, which is up here,
um, which is, which is capturing my voice.

00:05:47.234 --> 00:05:50.354
Um, that's linked
directly into the camera.

00:05:51.705 --> 00:05:56.175
I can see over here what's coming
up from the camera to make sure that

00:05:57.255 --> 00:05:58.545
it's still recording and I'm centered.

00:05:58.575 --> 00:06:05.655
And I also have two box lights up here,
one backlight and then four or five

00:06:05.655 --> 00:06:08.835
different lights in the background, um,
to, to light it up and see if you won't

00:06:08.835 --> 00:06:11.985
be able to see that right now, because
you're just staring at your camera.

00:06:12.225 --> 00:06:14.955
But when do you see the final
results on YouTube, it will

00:06:14.955 --> 00:06:15.885
hopefully look quite good.

00:06:18.540 --> 00:06:21.180
I'm afraid that some of my
viewers are going to say, why

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:22.869
don't you get a set up like Olly?

00:06:22.890 --> 00:06:24.570
Cause his is much better than yours.

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:25.770
So we'll see.

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:26.060
We're...

00:06:26.159 --> 00:06:26.700
it's...

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:30.180
in any case, we continue to
evolve and, and, and language

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:31.560
learning continues to evolve.

00:06:32.354 --> 00:06:35.534
Um, what do you think are
some of the major sort of

00:06:35.534 --> 00:06:37.155
changes that have taken place?

00:06:37.195 --> 00:06:37.794
Say...

00:06:38.034 --> 00:06:42.164
which Was the first sort of language
that you, other than English, your first

00:06:42.164 --> 00:06:43.995
foreign language, which was the first one?

00:06:44.354 --> 00:06:48.315
And if you compare how, uh, you
know, the world of language learning

00:06:48.315 --> 00:06:51.674
today to when you first started, what
have been some of the main changes?

00:06:51.705 --> 00:06:58.784
So my first language was French
and, um, I remember working in a

00:06:58.784 --> 00:07:02.955
cafe in London when I first, when
I first heard French around me and

00:07:02.955 --> 00:07:04.094
thought, well, that sounds fantastic.

00:07:04.094 --> 00:07:04.815
Let's go and learn it.

00:07:04.875 --> 00:07:08.085
And so on my way home, I would stop by the
European bookshop, which was then in...

00:07:08.085 --> 00:07:11.445
now it's in Gloucester road at the
time it was in Soho and I would go

00:07:11.445 --> 00:07:17.114
back and the only way that I knew
to learn French was with textbooks

00:07:17.114 --> 00:07:18.344
because that's what we did at school.

00:07:18.705 --> 00:07:22.995
And so I would go down into the European
bookshop, um, in Soho and I would go

00:07:22.995 --> 00:07:25.635
down and browse the French grammar books.

00:07:25.635 --> 00:07:29.025
You had The Complete Merde, which
was the perennial French book,

00:07:29.025 --> 00:07:30.645
which was there already at the time.

00:07:31.034 --> 00:07:33.765
And then there were the Collins
books, the Teach Yourself books.

00:07:33.765 --> 00:07:38.775
I don't, I don't remember what they
were and I would simply flick through

00:07:38.775 --> 00:07:42.630
the grammar books until I found one
that seemed more what I might enjoy.

00:07:42.690 --> 00:07:44.820
And then I would work
through it page by page.

00:07:45.570 --> 00:07:48.300
Um, and, uh, and that was pretty much it.

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:54.300
Now, um, fast forward 20 years or so...

00:07:54.570 --> 00:07:57.330
but subsequently you learn Spanish,
you learned Arabic, you learned

00:07:57.330 --> 00:07:58.240
a whole bunch of languages.

00:07:58.260 --> 00:07:58.560
Yeah.

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:01.950
And all of that was in the
kind of analog methods as well.

00:08:01.950 --> 00:08:05.370
So I think because I was,
I was trained in that way.

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:12.270
I've always found personally that
digitizing languages robs me of attention,

00:08:12.929 --> 00:08:15.450
which is for me as the most valuable
asset when it comes to the, any, anything.

00:08:15.450 --> 00:08:16.349
So I've always got...

00:08:16.349 --> 00:08:22.035
been, I've always been quite uh,
I've always preferred, you know,

00:08:22.065 --> 00:08:23.775
pencil and paper to anything else.

00:08:24.195 --> 00:08:25.665
The big things that have changed.

00:08:25.665 --> 00:08:29.505
I mean, the major ways that I use
technology now would be for, uh, for

00:08:29.505 --> 00:08:33.015
example, reading, reading Japanese,
I would read digitally always because

00:08:33.075 --> 00:08:36.465
it's just so much faster to look
up either the, you know, the read,

00:08:36.465 --> 00:08:39.765
the reading of a, of, of a Kanji
or the, or the meaning of a Kanji.

00:08:40.605 --> 00:08:45.225
Um, and then obviously I use the
internet to take language lessons, to

00:08:45.225 --> 00:08:51.935
connect with tutors and um, and just
for general, for general, I know, you

00:08:51.935 --> 00:08:55.545
know, watch movies, all the regular
stuff really, but that's about it.

00:08:55.545 --> 00:08:57.285
I'm not a big one for apps.

00:08:57.315 --> 00:08:58.245
I'm a big fan of LingQ.

00:08:58.245 --> 00:09:00.075
I use LingQ on a regular basis.

00:09:00.585 --> 00:09:04.905
Um, but I, but I, I very much,
I very much use the internet to

00:09:04.905 --> 00:09:08.265
connect today to speakers, to read
content, and then sometimes with a

00:09:08.265 --> 00:09:10.365
little bit of help when it gets hard.

00:09:12.495 --> 00:09:13.065
Right.

00:09:13.575 --> 00:09:15.135
Uh, interesting.

00:09:15.435 --> 00:09:21.105
I think it's true, and I agree with
you that reading on paper, uh, is

00:09:21.105 --> 00:09:23.295
better than reading on a computer.

00:09:24.675 --> 00:09:26.265
Uh, but there are a few conditions there.

00:09:26.325 --> 00:09:29.505
It's better because you, there
are fewer distractions and you are

00:09:29.505 --> 00:09:32.625
basically dealing with the words
and they're not shining at you.

00:09:32.625 --> 00:09:34.365
And it's, it's less distracting.

00:09:35.475 --> 00:09:40.920
However, I find that in my own case
I have to be at a certain level in

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:42.360
order to be able to read on paper.

00:09:43.140 --> 00:09:47.280
Uh, so if I take an extreme example
now, of course, when I learned Chinese,

00:09:47.490 --> 00:09:52.709
there were no computers and there was no
digital texts so it was all paper, but

00:09:52.709 --> 00:09:57.240
nowadays learning Persian and Arabic,
I'm just not strong enough in Arabic.

00:09:57.240 --> 00:10:01.230
I have your book by the way,
Arabic Short Stories, and I can

00:10:01.230 --> 00:10:05.790
read them, but it's tough because
the pronunciation is not obvious.

00:10:06.379 --> 00:10:08.880
Uh, the words that I
don't know, I don't know.

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:13.050
So if I can read it digitally,
I can hear the text to speech.

00:10:13.050 --> 00:10:14.160
I can look the word up.

00:10:14.459 --> 00:10:17.319
Um, I can see...

00:10:17.319 --> 00:10:18.599
in some languages.

00:10:18.599 --> 00:10:22.140
If I looked the word up, say on LingQ,
I can see a conjugating dictionary.

00:10:22.230 --> 00:10:25.980
I get a whole bunch of additional
information, which I find useful, but

00:10:25.980 --> 00:10:31.890
my goal is to get to a level where I can
read comfortably uh, a conventional book.

00:10:32.280 --> 00:10:35.819
And so even in terms of say Slavic
languages, it's easier for me to read

00:10:35.819 --> 00:10:40.470
Czech or Polish simply because the
alphabet that we're more used to is it's

00:10:40.470 --> 00:10:42.150
just that much easier for us to read.

00:10:42.660 --> 00:10:46.230
And even something like the Cyrillic
alphabet, which is really not that

00:10:46.230 --> 00:10:50.850
difficult, it's still just that little
bit more of a chore than reading in the

00:10:50.850 --> 00:10:52.650
alphabet that you're most familiar with.

00:10:53.310 --> 00:10:59.400
Uh, the other thing is, uh, if I go back
in history, the reason we started LingQ

00:10:59.400 --> 00:11:03.270
is because I had books at home in German
and in Spanish and there was always five,

00:11:03.270 --> 00:11:04.980
10 words per page that I didn't know.

00:11:04.980 --> 00:11:08.370
And I got tired of looking them up
in the dictionary, forgetting them...

00:11:08.430 --> 00:11:12.720
and so even with those languages where
let's say that there's more than 5%

00:11:12.750 --> 00:11:19.770
unknown words, I prefer now, even now
to read, uh, you know, digitally, uh, as

00:11:19.770 --> 00:11:22.170
my main means of acquiring these words.

00:11:22.590 --> 00:11:25.570
Uh, but for relaxation, even
though there are words, I don't

00:11:25.570 --> 00:11:27.360
know, I just skip over them.

00:11:27.540 --> 00:11:31.440
And I read them in a, in a traditional
book and I will get there by the

00:11:31.440 --> 00:11:32.950
way, with your Arabic Short Stories.

00:11:33.070 --> 00:11:33.760
I have no...

00:11:34.140 --> 00:11:35.400
I have no doubt that you will.

00:11:35.430 --> 00:11:40.050
Um, it's it's I think it's, I always
I'm left with the impression that

00:11:40.050 --> 00:11:44.670
when you have people such as yourself,
who, who already know how they learn

00:11:44.670 --> 00:11:47.880
languages best, and they have their
method that they've perfected over

00:11:47.880 --> 00:11:50.340
many, many years and many languages.

00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:55.020
Um, th th there's a very
specific set of, of conditions.

00:11:56.020 --> 00:11:59.190
And we, you know, we kind of, we
know what we like and we, we find

00:11:59.190 --> 00:12:00.780
ways to make, to make that work.

00:12:00.810 --> 00:12:02.220
We don't bend to the technology.

00:12:02.220 --> 00:12:04.470
We've got to make the technology
bend to us as, as it were.

00:12:04.950 --> 00:12:10.110
But I think for most people, really
the, I think this is, I think about

00:12:10.110 --> 00:12:14.250
it as a question of, of just peeling
back layers of difficulty one by one.

00:12:14.250 --> 00:12:18.900
And I think this is why technology
is interesting in the dev, how the

00:12:18.900 --> 00:12:23.730
development of technology is interesting
because, because I don't really know

00:12:23.730 --> 00:12:27.959
so much about the Apple, the technology
space, because, because I just

00:12:28.020 --> 00:12:29.400
simply don't use it so much myself.

00:12:29.640 --> 00:12:33.240
But when you, when it comes to content
and the kind of content that people are

00:12:33.240 --> 00:12:35.760
making, I find that very interesting.

00:12:35.849 --> 00:12:39.420
And I think the fact that people can
create content now distributed to a wide

00:12:39.420 --> 00:12:44.205
audience is very important because it
motivates them to do so, you know, so

00:12:44.205 --> 00:12:49.305
I could think of any number of examples
of content creators on YouTube who are

00:12:49.305 --> 00:12:53.355
now making content on, uh, based around
comprehensible input, for example.

00:12:53.535 --> 00:12:58.245
So this is something that we are obviously
big proponents of and is one of, of many

00:12:58.275 --> 00:13:00.825
different approaches to language learning,
but you've got people who are making a

00:13:00.825 --> 00:13:05.250
ton of videos teaching french or Japanese
or Spanish with comprehensible input.

00:13:05.250 --> 00:13:08.250
And they're using, they're using
objects, realea as it's, as it's known,

00:13:08.250 --> 00:13:10.400
they're using whiteboards, computer
screens, they're making these...

00:13:11.160 --> 00:13:14.790
everything that they're doing is making
the language more access, slightly

00:13:14.790 --> 00:13:16.830
more accessible, one layer at a time.

00:13:17.580 --> 00:13:22.260
And that's, I think where technology for
me seems to be really exciting is the

00:13:22.260 --> 00:13:26.430
evolution of these content platforms so
that someone can be a language creator on

00:13:26.430 --> 00:13:31.260
YouTube and then earn a living from doing
that, which means they can then make more.

00:13:31.260 --> 00:13:35.310
And we recently, we've seen this
huge proliferation in the amounts

00:13:35.310 --> 00:13:38.460
of useful content that's being
created and is out there for free.

00:13:38.910 --> 00:13:44.130
Um, and that's, uh, that's kind of,
uh, a hidden, a hidden way in which

00:13:44.310 --> 00:13:46.770
technology is, is driving things forward.

00:13:46.890 --> 00:13:50.490
You know, not so much at the, the, the
Apple software set in the industry,

00:13:50.490 --> 00:13:53.670
the enabling of people who are
just natural teachers to, to teach.

00:13:56.535 --> 00:13:58.064
Uh, I agree.

00:13:58.095 --> 00:14:02.835
And if we go into the sort of language
books section of any bookstore, whether it

00:14:02.835 --> 00:14:08.745
be in Tokyo or London, there's a limited
number of books that can be there, even if

00:14:08.745 --> 00:14:12.645
there's 10 different additions, you know,
Living Language and, uh, I don't know,

00:14:12.675 --> 00:14:16.905
uh, you know, any number of, it's not
going to be a hundred, it can only be the

00:14:16.905 --> 00:14:22.590
store can't possibly stock a hundred books
for learning these different languages,

00:14:22.740 --> 00:14:24.420
but there's no limit on the internet.

00:14:24.900 --> 00:14:26.280
There's no limit on YouTube.

00:14:26.880 --> 00:14:29.910
And so different people have
different ideas of how they can

00:14:29.910 --> 00:14:32.550
help people learn and they just put
them out to get feedback as well.

00:14:32.550 --> 00:14:34.080
The difficulty is actually finding them.

00:14:34.110 --> 00:14:37.530
You get that instant feedback so you
can, you can iterate very quickly.

00:14:37.650 --> 00:14:41.190
But when it comes to books, for example,
we, we recently just published a book

00:14:41.190 --> 00:14:48.045
of uh, Japanese short stories and it's
taken us, I think to well from when

00:14:48.045 --> 00:14:51.135
I first raised it with the publisher,
about three years to get it out.

00:14:51.705 --> 00:14:55.545
Um, you know, the length of time is
preposterous, really to get, to take a

00:14:55.545 --> 00:14:58.215
book from, I mean, there was probably
only a year, a year and a half.

00:14:58.355 --> 00:15:02.699
A year to a year and a half of actual
work, but, but when you think about

00:15:03.040 --> 00:15:05.760
that, we have one, we basically
have one shot to get it right.

00:15:05.939 --> 00:15:09.180
We spend a lot of time upfront and then we
put it out and we hope that it does well.

00:15:09.180 --> 00:15:10.829
And we hope that it's useful for people.

00:15:11.250 --> 00:15:16.230
Um, But if I were to do say, instead
of writing a book on Japanese stories

00:15:16.260 --> 00:15:18.449
instead to say, well, I'm going
to make a YouTube channel about

00:15:18.449 --> 00:15:20.459
Japanese in that year and a half.

00:15:20.699 --> 00:15:24.600
I could be so much more attuned to
what the concept of people actually

00:15:24.600 --> 00:15:27.930
like having listened to them, read
their comments, tried things out.

00:15:28.170 --> 00:15:31.290
So it's an incredibly
powerful, um, dynamic.

00:15:31.290 --> 00:15:33.420
It's all, it's almost like the
dynamic between a teacher and a

00:15:33.420 --> 00:15:36.030
student, you know, that, that instant,
that feedback loop that we get.

00:15:36.030 --> 00:15:36.900
Right.

00:15:37.990 --> 00:15:39.515
Feed back loop is important.

00:15:39.515 --> 00:15:42.135
And the other thing is just the
cost of getting it out there.

00:15:42.135 --> 00:15:46.575
If you spend three years getting
your Japanese storybook out there,

00:15:46.845 --> 00:15:49.875
you'd be hard pressed to get
10,000 people to buy that book.

00:15:49.875 --> 00:15:53.235
I don't know what your numbers are,
but my experience would tell me

00:15:53.235 --> 00:15:54.825
that that would be a large number.

00:15:55.455 --> 00:16:01.455
You can get a a hundred thousand
views of a video on YouTube and that

00:16:01.455 --> 00:16:02.715
you can make more and more videos.

00:16:02.715 --> 00:16:06.585
And so you, you know, th the, the
economies of scale are so much better.

00:16:07.455 --> 00:16:12.075
Um, one thing with YouTube, I'm a
great believer in YouTube, by the way,

00:16:12.105 --> 00:16:16.455
incidentally, as you know at LingQ, we
have a browser extension where we can

00:16:16.455 --> 00:16:22.545
automatically import YouTube videos with
the subtitles, the captions, and that...

00:16:22.755 --> 00:16:23.935
Ah I didn't know you could do that.

00:16:23.935 --> 00:16:25.145
That's fantastic.

00:16:25.814 --> 00:16:26.355
Oh yeah.

00:16:26.385 --> 00:16:27.285
I just click on it.

00:16:27.735 --> 00:16:31.074
Uh, you know, and I'm using things
like, uh, I dunno there's this...

00:16:31.505 --> 00:16:34.425
and that's another thing, you know,
content creation doesn't just have to

00:16:34.425 --> 00:16:39.765
be people like yourself or  or other
people who are creating great content.

00:16:40.395 --> 00:16:45.704
Uh, there's a Jordanian television, you
know, I follow the Jordanian television

00:16:45.704 --> 00:16:50.295
station in Levantine Arabic, and
there was also this sort of somewhat

00:16:50.295 --> 00:16:54.824
like a, you know, the Flintstones
type of cartoon, uh, in Jordanian,

00:16:54.824 --> 00:16:59.385
Arabic, which has, um, uh, you know,
subtitles in both English and Arabic.

00:16:59.885 --> 00:17:02.129
And so I can import that into LingQ.

00:17:02.189 --> 00:17:06.750
The Arabic becomes the text, but
I cannot actually follow the video

00:17:06.960 --> 00:17:09.720
and see the English subtitles
and then go through the text.

00:17:10.109 --> 00:17:12.150
And of course the text is divided.

00:17:12.180 --> 00:17:13.139
It's timestamped.

00:17:13.139 --> 00:17:14.280
So it sentence by sentence.

00:17:14.280 --> 00:17:17.910
I can go through listening to the cartoon
characters, say whatever they have

00:17:17.910 --> 00:17:20.940
to say word by sentence by sentence.

00:17:21.210 --> 00:17:23.250
Uh, but then I can also
watch that cartoon to get...

00:17:23.300 --> 00:17:24.599
the cartoon is three minutes.

00:17:25.454 --> 00:17:26.565
So this is content.

00:17:26.565 --> 00:17:28.185
This is tremendous learning content.

00:17:28.185 --> 00:17:32.565
So there is no limit to, I mean, I
could think of more examples like that

00:17:32.895 --> 00:17:36.955
and both Netflix and YouTube, where
you can bring these things into LingQ

00:17:36.975 --> 00:17:38.235
and use them as learning material.

00:17:38.355 --> 00:17:41.145
In fact, a lot of the stuff that
we're now, that I'm now seeing

00:17:41.145 --> 00:17:45.945
in both Persian and Arabic are
links to this kind of content.

00:17:45.975 --> 00:17:47.805
So it's not in our library.

00:17:48.525 --> 00:17:52.755
So it's not that I'm distributing it
to our members, but there was a link

00:17:52.755 --> 00:17:57.450
there that enables the member to go and
then download it into their account.

00:17:57.460 --> 00:18:00.540
So they are able to access
all of this, uh, content.

00:18:01.320 --> 00:18:03.250
Uh, so I mean, yeah, it's exploding.

00:18:03.890 --> 00:18:04.770
I have a question.

00:18:04.800 --> 00:18:05.100
Sure.

00:18:05.550 --> 00:18:07.380
Uh, grammar.

00:18:07.470 --> 00:18:12.180
Um, I was thinking about grammar the
other day, and I often feel that, uh,

00:18:12.210 --> 00:18:17.580
people are sort of taught grammar before
they have enough experience with the

00:18:17.580 --> 00:18:19.750
language so that the grammar becomes...

00:18:19.840 --> 00:18:23.805
it's difficult to understand what the
point is, uh, difficult to remember

00:18:23.805 --> 00:18:26.895
these rules much less apply them.

00:18:27.225 --> 00:18:31.335
However, I find that when I'm at a
certain level in the language, I do

00:18:31.335 --> 00:18:33.165
want to get closer to the grammar.

00:18:33.645 --> 00:18:35.985
I've had enough experience with
the language that I understand

00:18:36.015 --> 00:18:37.005
the points of trying to make.

00:18:37.005 --> 00:18:42.015
I've actually experienced it, you know,
and I feel that there is a lack of

00:18:42.045 --> 00:18:47.025
grammar texts in the target language,
not for beginners because beginners

00:18:47.025 --> 00:18:51.450
wouldn't be able to follow the grammatical
explanation, the language there that

00:18:51.450 --> 00:18:55.690
they're trying to learn, but when
you're at a kind of, almost like a B1

00:18:55.710 --> 00:19:02.640
plus almost B2 level, if there was,
um, a course in the target language

00:19:03.150 --> 00:19:08.270
about the grammar, not too long, audio
text texts can be imported into LingQ.

00:19:08.340 --> 00:19:09.990
Audio I can listen to in my car.

00:19:10.500 --> 00:19:12.060
I think that would be a great resource.

00:19:12.090 --> 00:19:13.140
Have you ever given that...

00:19:13.140 --> 00:19:14.560
so that those are not stories.

00:19:15.015 --> 00:19:19.485
The stories are the key, I agree with you,
uh, because the stories is sort of the

00:19:20.355 --> 00:19:23.455
enjoyment part of getting the language
into you, getting the brain use to the

00:19:23.475 --> 00:19:25.515
language, but we all want to be better.

00:19:25.515 --> 00:19:28.095
We want to be more accurate
in our use of the language.

00:19:28.465 --> 00:19:33.179
But if we could make the grammar
learning also meaningful input

00:19:33.209 --> 00:19:35.550
in the target language, I think
that would be of great value.

00:19:35.550 --> 00:19:39.870
Well, I mean, we do, we do almost exactly
that I'm at story learning actually.

00:19:39.870 --> 00:19:44.780
So what we have, and you can tell
me whether this is, um, whether I'm

00:19:44.790 --> 00:19:46.500
describing what you have in mind here.

00:19:46.500 --> 00:19:48.510
But so we, we run these
monthly challenges.

00:19:48.510 --> 00:19:52.830
So we have them uh they're, they are
30 days long, but basically, so what we

00:19:52.830 --> 00:19:57.540
do is we will take, uh, a grammar point
in a language, a tricky grammar point.

00:19:57.540 --> 00:20:00.630
So say for example, the difference
between the past tenses in Spanish,

00:20:00.630 --> 00:20:01.560
which is a perennial problem.

00:20:02.160 --> 00:20:07.660
And so we will write a story there's 30,
30 chapters, 30 days long based on...

00:20:08.290 --> 00:20:11.190
which focuses heavily
on that grammar point.

00:20:11.430 --> 00:20:15.540
And so the idea is that, um, so in this
case, you know, the two past tenses, and

00:20:15.540 --> 00:20:20.140
so the idea is that you spend 30 days
on a daily basis reading content that

00:20:20.300 --> 00:20:22.040
features that grammar point very heavily.

00:20:22.220 --> 00:20:27.230
Um, and then through by, by, by, by force
or input and repetition and frequency,

00:20:27.530 --> 00:20:30.860
um, you end up kind of developing
that natural, that natural feel.

00:20:31.130 --> 00:20:35.990
Um, and we find it works really well
because as you said, the barrier

00:20:35.990 --> 00:20:40.790
there really is, it's just, it's,
it's getting used to the way that that

00:20:40.790 --> 00:20:42.200
grammar works and it's developing.

00:20:42.200 --> 00:20:46.760
I mean, I can't tell you how often, how
often I've had this, this experience

00:20:46.760 --> 00:20:52.980
of, um, you know, thinking that I know
the grammar of a language fairly well,

00:20:54.090 --> 00:20:57.120
but never quite being able to use it in
speech and then going off and spending a

00:20:57.120 --> 00:21:00.480
year, reading in the language and coming
back and suddenly finding without having

00:21:00.480 --> 00:21:04.290
done any extra study that the grammar
just rolls off my tongue naturally, you

00:21:04.290 --> 00:21:05.880
know, more naturally than, than before.

00:21:06.090 --> 00:21:11.895
It's simply a question of, of exposure
over time, frequency and exposure.

00:21:11.895 --> 00:21:14.774
I mean, there really is no...

00:21:14.805 --> 00:21:15.135
I agree.

00:21:15.135 --> 00:21:18.735
No other elements of it, but it kind of,
that can be quite a tough, a tough pill

00:21:18.735 --> 00:21:24.125
to swallow for, um, for someone who's
coming at it new, because it's, it's

00:21:24.165 --> 00:21:31.034
not, it's never the case that you can
kind of chart your development or your

00:21:31.034 --> 00:21:33.945
progress against days or hours of study.

00:21:34.155 --> 00:21:39.585
You know, you just have, you just
have to, you know, dive in, surround

00:21:39.585 --> 00:21:42.855
yourself by it and then just trust
that, that we will then develop the,

00:21:43.395 --> 00:21:45.014
the knowledge as, as, as, as you go.

00:21:45.135 --> 00:21:45.555
Right.

00:21:47.205 --> 00:21:47.774
Yeah.

00:21:47.805 --> 00:21:52.125
I mean, uh, I think the brain,
we underestimate the brain, the

00:21:52.125 --> 00:21:57.044
brain has the capacity to establish
rules, create patterns, connections.

00:21:57.495 --> 00:22:01.065
And so I'll, after a year of
reading, you're going to naturally

00:22:01.185 --> 00:22:02.685
use the language more correctly.

00:22:03.300 --> 00:22:05.340
Uh, but some things you won't notice.

00:22:05.460 --> 00:22:08.820
And so we all know people who have
lived in Britain or Canada for

00:22:08.820 --> 00:22:12.120
30 years and still make the same
fundamental mistakes in English

00:22:12.120 --> 00:22:14.550
that reflect their native language.

00:22:15.150 --> 00:22:17.520
Uh, but they're still getting more...

00:22:17.520 --> 00:22:20.610
or they've reached a certain level
that much of what they say in

00:22:20.610 --> 00:22:21.960
English is grammatically correct.

00:22:22.560 --> 00:22:24.150
Uh, so the brain will form patterns.

00:22:24.915 --> 00:22:28.605
Uh, and to that extent, I agree with
you that lots of listening, reading is

00:22:28.605 --> 00:22:32.685
going to help the brain farm patterns
that are increasingly correct, but

00:22:32.685 --> 00:22:34.065
there are things that we don't notice.

00:22:34.575 --> 00:22:38.475
And I think when we start into
the language, some sort of overall

00:22:38.505 --> 00:22:41.775
description of how the language
works in English is kind of helpful.

00:22:41.775 --> 00:22:44.445
And as long as we don't try to
remember any of it, cause we can't.

00:22:45.135 --> 00:22:46.755
Uh, but it kind of helps us.

00:22:46.785 --> 00:22:49.605
Although certain things are immediately
obvious, the verb comes at the end.

00:22:49.605 --> 00:22:52.635
You almost don't need to be told that
you'll figure that out pretty quick.

